{
  "dom_id": "step-q334210",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{\u2032}},\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\ne 0.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{\u2032}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\frac{1}{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & ={(xp+\\frac{1}{p})}^{\u2032} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{\u2032}-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}}{p}^{\u2032} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{\u2032}(x-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{\u2032}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c\\ne 0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\frac{1}{p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\frac{1}{c}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c\\ne 0.$",
